#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 540.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139365} :activity/id #uuid "2320ac0f-1ba4-5fab-8b23-0557556abfdc" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050076 :topic/id #uuid "2b568414-117a-54db-9c62-047f1acb1909" :topic/math-academy-id 2230} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139365 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139596 :tutorial/content "The formula for the speed $S$ of a moving object is\n\n$$\nS=\\dfrac{D}{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the distance covered, and $T$ is the time taken.\n\nFor example, if a car travels a distance of $600\\,\\textrm{m}$ within a time of $30\\,\\textrm{s},$ at constant speed, then we can find the speed of the car using the formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =\\frac{D}{T} \\\\  & =\\frac{600}{30} \\\\  & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBut what is the unit of speed? We can find it by substituting the units for distance and time into the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =\\frac{D}{T} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{meters}}{\\text{seconds}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the speed of the car is $20\\,\\textrm{m}/\\textrm{s}.$ When the unit has a quotient like this, we say \"meters *per* second.\"" :tutorial/id #uuid "61193d89-1b00-531f-be9f-18801cc08970" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1399 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "2c2e40d2-0cf6-5448-9925-a2a8ae44cc69" :step/math-academy-id 3018 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139367}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139367 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138437 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138310 :question/id #uuid "36127b89-97ba-5f98-bd8e-b36ae1a261f4" :question/math-academy-id "e-896"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e447dc49-3549-568c-ade5-86601beeb3a6" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049253} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051057}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138795} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138797} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496261} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496319} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496331}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Units When There Is No Cancellation"} :step/id #uuid "2bfae739-4b1c-551e-8e72-be87c28b1bef" :step/math-academy-id 3019 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139368}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139368 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138438 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138311 :question/id #uuid "ee3fa65e-1146-598a-a89d-33d287e88066" :question/math-academy-id "e-897"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e1b4e78-8b4a-59ea-8fac-9092c2eeb67a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050843} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051029}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138793} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139082} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496290} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496329} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558530} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558558}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Units When There Is Cancellation"} :step/id #uuid "82c13ded-0a7f-5183-b153-dbfb81baff87" :step/math-academy-id 3022 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139369}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139369 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139597 :tutorial/content "Sometimes, we can be given a formula with values whose units are not the standard ones we know.\n\nFor instance, suppose the formula for a person's popularity $P$ is given by\n\n$$\nP=\\dfrac{2f}{s},\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ is the number of friends that person has, and $s$ is the number of siblings they have.\n\nIf we want to find the unit of popularity, then we need to substitute the units for the number of friends ($f$) and the number of siblings ($s$) into the formula.\n\n- The units for the number of friends is '$\\textrm{friends}$' since we say 'I have $5$ friends'.\n\n- Similarly, the units for the number of siblings is '$\\textrm{siblings}.$'\n\nSubstituting these units into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\frac{2f}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot \\text{friends}}{\\text{siblings}} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\text{friends}}{\\text{siblings}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the appropriate unit is $\\dfrac{\\textrm{friends}}{\\textrm{siblings}}$, or \"friends per sibling.\" Here we ignore the constant $2$ since it does not affect the units." :tutorial/id #uuid "ab30765e-d9e3-53d5-a050-8bd889054974" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1400 :tutorial/title "Formulas With Non-Standard Units"} :step/id #uuid "976f91da-bd0c-5ab2-bca4-a77eb3665217" :step/math-academy-id 3024 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139370}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139370 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138439 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138312 :question/id #uuid "b0212d4c-eb7a-5cec-8eb1-1c60be355f4e" :question/math-academy-id "e-898"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "deb16829-8ec8-5cbb-b81f-2c526bce165e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138796} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139083} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496231} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496334} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558559} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558574}] :knowledge-point/title "Dividing Nonstandard Units"} :step/id #uuid "ecd1aeef-36e3-51c0-8e9f-f64384ff00d8" :step/math-academy-id 3025}] :activity/title "Determining Units in Formulas" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186138590}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558693} :activity/id #uuid "c9887f59-1c5a-5806-9f1e-3964e8169168" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050076 :topic/id #uuid "2b568414-117a-54db-9c62-047f1acb1909" :topic/math-academy-id 2230} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558588 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558530 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13280df4-2330-5e3f-a795-a4797d385b73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557910} {:answer/id #uuid "92e3a44a-45f8-56a5-93ce-aadc94b4a4b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{s}}{\\text{m}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558153} {:answer/id #uuid "aac98899-8115-5802-b963-acb140b5967b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558204} {:answer/id #uuid "e59368b3-7a79-555b-bf69-9f407a1f2366" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\text{s}}^{2}}{{\\text{m}}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558326} {:answer/id #uuid "e8401ebb-283d-5b15-a0db-c2b75fab6b15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{s}}{{\\text{m}}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558331}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557910} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8d533bf-32da-572b-b69a-5759fa85571d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558475}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "7753ef82-1930-5153-93dc-89e284e045d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-46152" :question/problem "The speed of a car moving with a constant acceleration is given by the formula $v=at,$ where $a$ is the car's acceleration, and $t$ is the time passed. Given that the acceleration is measured in $\\frac{\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}$, determine the units of speed in terms of meters and seconds." :question/worked-solution "Acceleration is measured in $\\frac{\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}$ and time is measured in seconds. We can find the unit of speed by substituting the given units of acceleration and time into the formula and simplifying. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =a\\cdot t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}\\cdot \\text{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{m}\\,\\cancel{\\text{s}}}{{\\text{s}}^{\\cancel{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the unit of speed is $\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}}.$"} :step/id #uuid "11ad8382-e548-54e8-8797-9de5e2d6db1f"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558681 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558558 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0087c9b5-6fa9-54cf-b875-8154f8e1a3e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}\\,{\\text{km}}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557870} {:answer/id #uuid "16b3c8b4-9bbf-5a82-b8c2-9a041a21c8b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{km}\\,{\\text{s}}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557920} {:answer/id #uuid "21442043-31fd-5aab-92ba-0f264502b9eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\text{s}}^{2}}{\\text{m}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557937} {:answer/id #uuid "57ec2723-24ad-548e-bbe6-9d7bd87f30f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558032} {:answer/id #uuid "6c26b31a-a05b-53fb-b0ed-1793466f9725" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558077}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558077} :answer-field/id #uuid "11e0e1ea-dc6c-5b3f-89da-7dc4c90d0d81" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558369}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b8a0ff68-2bfe-50b7-8470-09b73dbea458" :question/math-academy-id "q-46095" :question/problem "The acceleration $a$ of a body is related to applied force $F$ and mass $m$ by the equation $a=\\frac{F}{m}.$ If $F$ is measured in Newtons $(\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}})$ and $m$ is measured in kilograms, determine an appropriate measurement unit for acceleration." :question/worked-solution "We can find the unit of acceleration by substituting the given units of force and mass into the formula and simplifying.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{F}{m} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}})}{\\text{kg}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{kg}}\\,\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}\\,\\cancel{\\text{kg}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the unit of acceleration is $\\frac{\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}.$"} :step/id #uuid "ba3c538d-4be0-5fa1-aad7-7eb962e8f9ec" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558588}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558693 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558574 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9df49a9f-7b13-5d0e-94bf-4077f92c9eef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{machines}}{\\text{ball}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558179} {:answer/id #uuid "adc47bce-7d5b-5419-90c1-b6900875bc26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\text{balls}}^{2}}{\\text{machine}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558214} {:answer/id #uuid "d44676fb-cfb9-5576-bfd7-4326efdf4071" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{balls}}{\\text{machine}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558284} {:answer/id #uuid "db400d60-85d5-52eb-959c-6924766f7a85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\text{machines}}^{2}}{{\\text{ball}}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558299} {:answer/id #uuid "e6f489c1-6d75-5a3e-8fdf-791aab447eda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\text{machines}}^{2}}{\\text{ball}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558327}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558284} :answer-field/id #uuid "d31a35ae-5ca8-5d6c-a47c-8b5b084ab4be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558460}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f55788bf-5fa6-5009-a74f-4343731f157e" :question/math-academy-id "q-46070" :question/problem "A factory with $150$ machines produces $600\\,000$ baseball balls weekly. To determine the number of balls produced by a machine on average, the owner of the factory uses the formula\n\n$$\nP=\\frac{b}{m},\n$$\n\n \nwhere $b$ is the number of balls produced by the factory weekly, and $m$ is the number of machines in the factory.\n\nWhat is an appropriate measurement unit for $P?$" :question/worked-solution "We are solving for measurement units, so we can ignore the quantities $150$ and $600\\,000.$ They do not matter, if we are just solving for units.\n\nSince $b$ represents $\\text{balls}$ and $m$ represents $\\text{machines},$ the appropriate measurement unit for $P=\\frac{b}{m}$ is $\\frac{\\text{balls}}{\\text{machines}}.$"} :step/id #uuid "d27593e5-46d5-5615-a52c-7eea72d15309" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558681}}] :activity/title "Determining Units in Formulas" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557866}]}
